===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To surround with water; make into an island.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1933|author=Harriet Monroe|journal=Poetry|volume=42
|passage=We paused at little river cities along the way and walked upon their bushy dikes, and heard tales of overflows in flood seasons, when four feet or more of water '''islanded''' the houses.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1956|year_published=1972|author=w:Anthony Burgess|title=Time for a Tiger|series=The Malayan Trilogy|page=138|passage=The car soon seemed '''islanded''' in water.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To set, dot (as if) with islands.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=T. H. Chivers|chapter=The Song of Seralim: A Celestial Melologue|title=Magnolia; Or Southern Appalachian|year=1842|volume=II, number 1|page=52|passage=God dwells in light! Before the ocean of unmeasured space, was '''islanded''' with stars serenely bright, reflecting back the radiance of his face - he dwelt above in heaven’s immortal bliss, thinking into existence that which is.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1915|author=[[w:Edward Hutton (writer)|Edward Hutton]]|title=Naples and Southern Italy|page=22|passage=This Apulia is a land of vast pastures and cornfields and olive gardens, '''islanded''' with many rich cities, notable if only for the splendour of their churches,{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1930|author=w:Rupert Hughes|title=Ladies’ Man|page=143|passage=She knew that the town was '''islanded''' with many waters - the Hudson, the Harlem and the East rivers, and the Bay - but the rivers were hard to find in the universal velvetiness.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[isolate]].
#* {{RQ:Whitney Gayworthys|chapter=The Secret at the Hartshornes|page=49|passage=The turkey's nest was '''islanded''' with a fragrant swath,—the "heft" of the crop noted and rejoiced over,—the tons of good timothy and clover calculated,— {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Housman Shropshire Lad|poem=XXVII|passage=High the vanes of Shrewsbury gleam / '''Islanded''' in Severn stream.}}

